    {
      "key": "essay",
      "id": "in_6848",
      "label": "Why do you want to study this programme?",
      "page": "p5",
      "type": "essay",
      "required": true,
      "idStyle": "opaque",
      "kind": "textarea",
      "text": "I first met distributed systems through failure: a campus course-selection site that collapsed every semester. Rebuilding its queueing layer in my second year taught me that reliability is a design property, not an afterthought. Since then I have taken Operating Systems, Algorithms and Machine Learning, and spent an exchange semester at Kyoto University studying stream processing. During my internship at Formosa Cloud Systems I built a log-anomaly detector that now supports the weekly on-call review. These experiences left me with one question I cannot answer alone: how can a learning system stay trustworthy when the data it watches keeps changing? A taught master's programme with strong systems and machine-learning groups is the right place to pursue it. I hope to contribute a practitioner's curiosity, careful engineering habits, and the patience to measure before I claim.",
      "asciiOnly": true
    },
